# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.coverage*
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Throwaway working area, one per checkout. Scratch scripts, probes, and any
# environment built for a one-off task live here and nowhere else — see the
# Temporary Files rule in project-instructions.md. Several checkouts of this
# repository share one disk, and the system temp directory is shared with the
# whole machine; a ~1 GB unsigned install there was blocked as a dropper by the
# host's antivirus, correctly, and drop-then-execute is what trips endpoint
# protection. E006 and E007 added this rule independently, from the same
# cause on different machines; the two entries are merged here into one.
.tmp/

# Caches belonging to libraries that do NOT resolve through `tempfile`, so
# TMPDIR/TEMP/TMP never reach them. PyTensor and Numba each keep their own
# compile cache, and left alone PyTensor writes to %LOCALAPPDATA%\PyTensor —
# outside the checkout entirely. The Temporary Files rule now pins both into
# `.tmp/`; these patterns are the second line, for the failure that already
# happened: a `TMPDIR` handed to PyTensor in a form it could not parse was
# flattened into the *relative* directory
# `src/model/claudecodeKayaDemoProcurementRisk1.tmppytensor/`, created inside
# the source tree, and committed. A redirect that silently degrades to a
# relative path is worse than none, because it looks like it worked.
compiledir_*/
.pytensor/
.numba_cache/
# A flattened redirect lands as a directory named for the checkout path with its
# separators stripped, so the suffix varies with whatever the caller passed:
# `…Risk1.tmppytensor/` and `…Risk2.tmppt2/` have both occurred. Match the shape,
# not the two instances — the first version of this rule named only the suffix
# that had already happened and missed the next one four commits later.
*.tmp*/

# ADR-0015's local spool. It is a runtime artifact of a gateway invocation, one
# per working copy, and was committed once by a `git add -A` that swept it up.
gateway-invocation-spool.sqlite3

# Node
node_modules/
.next/
next-env.d.ts

# Agent tooling — kept local. The workflow that produced this repository is not
# part of what it delivers, so the skills, subagent definitions, and the
# agent-context files stay untracked.
/.claude/
.claudeignore
/.github/agents/
/.github/instructions/
/.github/skills/
/.github/sddp-config.md
/AGENTS.md
/CLAUDE.md

# `specs/` and `project-instructions.md` are deliberately tracked. The
# specification, plan, ADRs, checklists, and analysis report record why this
# scaffold is shaped the way it is — which is most of what makes the repository
# worth reading. Do not re-add them here.

# Local backup of pre-rewrite git history (created during history scrub)
/.git.backup-pre-scrub/

# Compose reads this automatically and it holds machine-specific host-port
# overrides (PRC_DB_PORT and friends). Several checkouts of this repository can
# share one machine, so the right value differs per working copy and must never
# be committed.
/.env

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

/gitignored/

# The gateway's local write-ahead spool (ADR-0015, TR-041). It is created in the
# working directory of whichever process invokes the gateway, so an ingestion run
# through the modeling entry drops one at `src/model/`. It holds only
# unreconciled invocation records and drains to empty, which is what keeps it
# outside the "no second datastore of record" rule (project-instructions v1.2.1)
# — and a committed one would be a snapshot of exactly the records that had not
# yet reached Postgres.
gateway-invocation-spool.sqlite3
